# Two top-level `fn`s with the same name in one module. The front end accepted
# this outright: `--phase check` reported OK and `--emit-ir` exited 0, emitting
# TWO `define` blocks for one symbol. The only thing that ever objected was
# `opt --passes=verify` ("invalid redefinition of function 'twice'") — an LLVM
# error naming a mangled symbol, with no source position and no mention of
# either declaration. On the ordinary compile path that invalid IR is handed
# straight to clang.
#
# Found while implementing the instance-head check (PR #108): a new helper was
# written byte-identical to one already 3000 lines up in infer.sprout, and the
# SEED REFRESH is what reported it.
fn twice(x: Int) -> Int = x + 1

fn twice(x: Int) -> Int = x + 1

fn main() -> Unit !{IO} = print(twice(1))
